# Project: Trip Sheet, Fuel Management & Driver Payroll System

Act as a senior product designer, UX architect, and full-stack engineer. Build a professional, production-oriented web application for a transport and fleet management company.

The application must manage the complete operational and financial lifecycle of drivers, vehicles, trip sheets, fuel consumption, trip expenses, driver advances, fuel deductions, monthly payroll, and payslip generation.

Do not build a generic CRUD dashboard. Build a cohesive fleet operations and financial management application with a clear workflow, realistic data, detailed tables, usable forms, dashboards, validation, and business-rule-driven calculations.

First, establish the complete frontend structure and functional user interface. Organize the application so a real backend and PostgreSQL database can be integrated without rewriting the business logic.

---

# 1. PRODUCT OBJECTIVE

The application must support this end-to-end workflow:

1. Create and maintain driver profiles.
2. Assign salary structures to drivers.
3. Record advances issued to drivers.
4. Record fuel top-ups for vehicles on a separate page.
5. Create and manage individual trip sheets.
6. Record trip start and end odometer readings or manually reported distance.
7. Record fuel consumed by each trip and calculate trip mileage.
8. Record multiple route segments within one trip.
9. Record and approve trip expenses.
10. Reconcile trip advances against approved trip expenses.
11. Maintain outstanding driver advance balances.
12. Calculate potential fuel deductions against configurable mileage benchmarks.
13. Review and approve fuel deductions before payroll.
14. Generate monthly payroll using applicable salary structures and approved financial adjustments.
15. Generate individual driver payslips.

The system must also integrate with the existing TLCM Pro Tyre Lifecycle Management System through its backend API.

The Trip Sheet application will use a separate PostgreSQL database. It must not directly write to the TLCM Pro database.

