# 10. TRIP SHEET MANAGEMENT

This is the central module of the application.

Create a dedicated trip list, trip creation workflow, trip detail page, and trip completion workflow.

The interface must make it easy to record a trip while preserving accurate odometer, fuel, expense, and financial information.

## Trip list page

Columns:

* Trip number.
* Trip date.
* Driver.
* Vehicle.
* Starting location.
* Final destination.
* Starting odometer.
* Ending odometer.
* Trip distance.
* Fuel consumed.
* Calculated mileage.
* Trip status.
* Odometer validation status.
* Actions.

Filters:

* Date range.
* Driver.
* Vehicle.
* Trip status.
* Odometer status.
* Expense approval status.
* Fuel deduction status.

Provide a prominent "Create Trip" button.

## Trip creation workflow

Use a structured multi-section form or a stepper with clear progress.

Recommended sections:

1. Trip Details.
2. Odometer and Distance.
3. Route Segments.
4. Fuel Consumption.
5. Trip Expenses.
6. Advance Settlement.
7. Fuel Deduction.
8. Review and Complete.

Users must be able to save a trip as a draft and resume it later.

Do not require every section to be complete before saving a draft.

### Section A: Trip details

Fields:

* Trip number, generated by the system.
* Driver.
* Vehicle.
* Trip start date and time.
* Trip end date and time, entered when completing the trip.
* Trip purpose or reference.
* Notes.

A trip should normally have one primary vehicle and one primary driver.

Record driver and vehicle assignment history so that later changes do not rewrite historical trip attribution.

### Section B: Odometer and distance

At trip start, capture:

* Starting odometer reading.
* Reading timestamp.
* Reading source.
* Validation status.

At trip completion, capture:

* Ending odometer reading.
* Reading timestamp.
* Reading source.

The user must also be able to enter the reported trip distance when one or both odometer readings are unavailable.

Support these cases:

CASE 1: Both readings available.

Starting odometer: 125,000 km.
Ending odometer: 125,450 km.
Calculated distance: 450 km.

CASE 2: Starting reading available, ending reading unavailable.

Starting odometer: 125,000 km.
Reported distance: 450 km.
Provisional derived ending reading: 125,450 km.

Mark the derived reading as derived, not measured.

CASE 3: Odometer readings unavailable.

Reported distance: 450 km.
Absolute odometer readings: unavailable.

The trip can still be used for operational reporting, but the system must not fabricate absolute readings.

CASE 4: Odometer sequence is inconsistent.

Flag the discrepancy and require review.

Do not silently rewrite readings or clamp values to make them appear valid.

Keep the following concepts separate:

* Measured distance.
* Reported distance.
* Derived distance.
* Validated distance.
* Distance eligible for tyre attribution.

A trip distance may be accepted for driver and trip analytics while tyre-distance attribution remains on hold until odometer validation is complete.

### Section C: Repeatable route segments

Within a single trip, allow the user to add any number of route segments.

Each segment contains:

* Sequence number.
* From location.
* To location.
* Departure time, if available.
* Arrival time, if available.
* Segment distance, if known.
* Notes.

Example:

1. Chennai → Vellore.
2. Vellore → Bengaluru.
3. Bengaluru → Hosur.
4. Hosur → Chennai.

Provide an "Add Route Segment" button.

Allow users to edit, reorder where appropriate, and remove segments before finalization.

Do not limit a trip to one From-to pair.

Avoid double-counting distance when both trip-level distance and route-segment distances are available.

### Section D: Trip fuel consumption

Provide fields for:

* Fuel consumed during the trip, in litres.
* Fuel measurement method.
* Fuel calculation status.
* Opening fuel quantity, when known.
* Fuel added during the trip, when known.
* Closing fuel quantity, when known.
* Notes.

When reliable fuel-balance information is available:

Fuel Consumed = Opening Fuel + Fuel Added − Closing Fuel

When the user enters fuel consumption manually, preserve the manually reported value.

If calculated consumption and manually reported consumption differ, flag the discrepancy for review.

Calculate:

Trip Mileage = Accepted Trip Distance ÷ Accepted Fuel Consumed

Display mileage in km/litre.

If distance or fuel consumption is missing or invalid, show "Unavailable" or "Pending verification" rather than displaying a misleading result.

### Section E: Trip expenses

Allow multiple expenses within a trip.

Expense fields:

* Expense category.
* Description.
* Amount.
* Expense date.
* Payment method.
* Receipt or attachment.
* Submitted by.
* Approval status.
* Approved amount.
* Reviewer.
* Review notes.

Example categories:

* Refreshments.
* RTO charges.
* Tolls.
* Parking.
* Loading and unloading.
* Repairs.
* Other operational expenses.

Provide an "Add Expense" button.

Display:

* Total submitted expenses.
* Total approved expenses.
* Total rejected expenses.
* Expenses awaiting approval.

Only approved expenses should be used in advance settlement.

Do not silently approve every entered expense.

### Section F: Advance settlement

Display advances associated with the trip.

Support multiple advances against the same trip.

Calculate:

Outstanding Settlement Balance =
Total Advances Issued
− Total Approved Expenses
− Amount Returned
− Other Valid Settlement Credits, if configured.

For example:

Advance issued: ₹10,000.
Approved expenses: ₹1,600.
Amount returned: ₹0.
Outstanding balance: ₹8,400.

Show the calculation clearly.

Provide actions to:

* Record an advance.
* Allocate an advance to the trip.
* Record money returned by the driver.
* View settlement history.
* Submit settlement for review.
* Approve or reject settlement.

Do not automatically treat an outstanding balance as a payroll deduction.

If the company authorizes recovery through payroll, create a separate approved recovery record.

### Section G: Fuel deduction

Include a dedicated fuel analysis and deduction section on the trip detail page.

Display:

* Accepted distance.
* Accepted fuel consumption.
* Actual mileage.
* Applicable mileage benchmark.
* Applicable tolerance.
* Expected fuel consumption.
* Allowed fuel consumption.
* Excess fuel consumption.
* Applicable fuel price.
* Potential deduction.
* Calculation status.
* Review status.

Use the configured mileage benchmark applicable to the vehicle and trip conditions.

Formula:

Expected Fuel = Accepted Distance ÷ Benchmark Mileage

If tolerance is expressed as a permitted excess percentage:

Allowed Fuel = Expected Fuel × (1 + Tolerance Percentage)

Excess Fuel = MAX(0, Accepted Fuel − Allowed Fuel)

Potential Deduction = Excess Fuel × Applicable Fuel Price

Example:

Distance: 450 km.
Benchmark: 6 km/litre.
Tolerance: 5%.
Accepted fuel consumption: 90 litres.
Fuel price: ₹95/litre.

Expected fuel: 75 litres.
Allowed fuel: 78.75 litres.
Excess fuel: 11.25 litres.
Potential deduction: ₹1,068.75.

The example values are illustrative.

The interface must distinguish between:

* Calculated estimate.
* Pending review.
* Approved deduction.
* Rejected deduction.
* Adjusted deduction.
* Posted to payroll.
* Recovered through payroll.

Allow an authorized reviewer to adjust a proposed deduction with a mandatory reason.

Never automatically post an unapproved fuel deduction to payroll.

Preserve the original calculation and adjustment history.

### Section H: Trip completion

Provide a review summary before the user completes a trip.

Display:

* Driver and vehicle.
* Trip dates.
* Route segments.
* Odometer readings.
* Accepted distance.
* Fuel consumption and mileage.
* Expenses and approval status.
* Advances and settlement balance.
* Fuel deduction status.
* TLCM Pro integration status.

Allow trip completion even if some financial or odometer tasks remain unresolved, provided the business rules allow it.

Represent operational completion, financial settlement, odometer validation, and tyre-distance attribution using separate statuses.

Do not treat "Trip Completed" as equivalent to "Financially Settled."

